export const body = "<p>Chapter 04: Visual Composition</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>As said in Chapter 2, we will focus on first- and third-person cameras. That means we will compose dynamic 3D spaces instead of static 2D images. If it helps, you can think of 3D composition in terms of interior design, instead of painting.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/87a190ba761a8505580c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/87a190ba761a8505580c.webp\" width=\"878\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:525px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Contrast</h2>\n\n<p>Contrast simply means <em>different,</em> and <em>different</em> attracts attention. While we don’t have the ability to frame scenes during gameplay like we do in movies, we can create points of contrast in our environments to tell players where they should look.</p>\n\n<p>For example, if there are many rectanglular objects in the level, a round object will immediately grab the player’s attention. And shapes are only one of many things we can use to create contrast.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7bb3b23ddf49fa3f87dc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7bb3b23ddf49fa3f87dc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"991\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong> <strong>– Contrast</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Breath of the Wild </em>uses triangular shapes for its landmarks to make them contrast against the relatively plain terrain. Likewise, <em>Control</em> uses an inverted pyramid to attract the player amidst the cubic geometry of brutalist architecture.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/20346299c35c3806125d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/20346299c35c3806125d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Similarly, in these two examples, we have the contrast of scale.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c710e49623a4812a9e0b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c710e49623a4812a9e0b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"357\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In these pictures, we have the contrast between two complementary colors: blue and orange. In the left picture, blue is more common throughout the scene, so it makes orange stand out. In the right picture, orange is more common, so blue gets the spotlight instead.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0d2c1ab3501778a7dba1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0d2c1ab3501778a7dba1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"298\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Continuing on the topic of brightness, players are always attracted by light.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6e8c32a4d075213a8858.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6e8c32a4d075213a8858.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Balance</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>When we look at a horizontal image, we tend to divide it into two halves. After the division, we also tend to compare the left half and the right half, trying to spot the differences. This tendency results in visual balance.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a089cabf85bc2f94e9b7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a089cabf85bc2f94e9b7.webp\" width=\"778\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:467px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Visual weight</h3>\n\n<p>Balancing an image is about managing visual weight. After splitting the screen, you can make each half look the same and, thus, appear balanced. Or, you can make each half look distinct, but with equal visual weight, so that they also appear balanced.</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Heavy visual weight</strong>: large scale, detailed, dark color, intensive color, heavy contrast</li><li><strong>Light visual weight</strong>: small scale, stark appearance, light color, soft color, light contrast</li></ul>\n\n<p>This leads us to two main types of balance: <strong>symmetric balance </strong>and <strong>asymmetric balance</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/21f86bf7f55d38a3cc1b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/21f86bf7f55d38a3cc1b.webp\" width=\"729\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:438px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Symmetric balance</h3>\n\n<p>Symmetric balance means the left half and the right half are similar. They don’t have to be identical, but they should look similar enough to create a sense of visual unity. Because of this unity, symmetrical balance feels calm and serene.</p>\n\n<p>A scene built with symmetry is good at leading players through because it looks like a hallway.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/24c2b1f9b360ce280265.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/24c2b1f9b360ce280265.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Asymmetric balance</h3>\n\n<p>As the name suggests, asymmetric balance is the opposite of symmetric balance. That means, after splitting the screen, we populate each half with different elements to create contrast.</p>\n\n<p>For a scene to remain balanced with asymmetry, we need to maintain equal visual weight. Therefore, if we have one big object on the left, we should place several small objects on the right.</p>\n\n<p>Asymmetric balance results in more complex scenes. Thus, it is a great tool to ask players to slow down and explore their surroundings.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b5d12d0536d0b1ee5fd7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b5d12d0536d0b1ee5fd7.webp\" width=\"939\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:561px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Negative space</h2>\n\n<p>To understand negative space, it helps to learn about positive space first. Every picture needs a focal point, a character or an object. That focal point is positive space, the space that is intended for the viewer to focus on.</p>\n\n<p>In contrast, negative space is the space that isn’t intended for the viewer to focus on. It is a background element meant to elevate the focal point. By narrowing the viewer’s attention to a specific region in the image, we can produce a more intentional and visually pleasing composition. Because if everything fights for attention, then nothing gets the attention.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bedeaddbb03577d461f2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bedeaddbb03577d461f2.webp\" width=\"864\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:514px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>With</strong><strong> enough negative space</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Diablo 2</em> is an isometric action role-playing game (ARPG) where the main gameplay loop revolves around hacking and slashing horde upon horde of enemies. For that reason, the ground textures are kept simple to allow the action and special effects to be the focal point. (Screenshots from the 2020 remake.)</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4df5a39e091a22eaf949.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4df5a39e091a22eaf949.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Without</strong><strong> enough negative space</strong></p>\n\n<p>Here is <em>The Ascent</em>, another isometric ARPG similar to <em>Diablo</em> <em>2</em>. The game takes place in a fictional universe inspired by the cyberpunk aesthetic. Although bursting neons and dirty metals are among the visual traits of the genre, <em>The Ascent</em> packs too much information in its environments, making them look less readable as a result.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4ee2adedadb1e97d13a7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4ee2adedadb1e97d13a7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Negative space</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Shadow of the Colossus </em>uses negative space to convey emptiness and emphasize the size difference between the player and their foes.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0216247c050e01fee57b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0216247c050e01fee57b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"432\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c42883c4eeca06443cdd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c42883c4eeca06443cdd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/304ca020357cb8d78537.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/304ca020357cb8d78537.webp\" width=\"1140\" height=\"490\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fb7b243a2705bb46a4dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fb7b243a2705bb46a4dd.webp\" width=\"638\" height=\"360\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/30d48a0337c0dd6686ee.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/30d48a0337c0dd6686ee.webp\" width=\"638\" height=\"360\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/83144d152241f480583b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/83144d152241f480583b.webp\" width=\"638\" height=\"360\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Line</h2>\n\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n\n<p>Lines communicate direction and length. They are like a visual handhold, guiding our eyes throughout the image to arrive at the focal point intended by the artist.</p>\n\n<p>We will look at:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Vertical lines</strong></li><li><strong>Horizontal lines</strong></li><li><strong>Curved lines</strong></li><li><strong>Leading lines</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p>Keep in mind that the information provided here is just a guideline, so you don’t have to adhere to it all the time.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f672e77765762e8e5a3c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f672e77765762e8e5a3c.webp\" width=\"873\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:524px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Vertical lines</h3>\n\n<p>Vertical lines convey scale and power. Lofty structures, natural or man-made, add an imposing quality to the environment as they loom over the player with every step they make forward.</p>\n\n<p>Vertical lines are also a good way to nudge the player to look skyward.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0d4acaf585c32839bda6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0d4acaf585c32839bda6.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Horizontal lines</h3>\n\n<p>Horizontal lines add a serene quality to the scenery because everything is at eye level. Having these lines in an environment, indoors or outdoors, makes it feel calmer and slower, allowing the player to breathe.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/46b0c60eb41084cfdd2d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/46b0c60eb41084cfdd2d.webp\" width=\"883\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Curved lines</h3>\n\n<p>Of the 3 types so far, curved lines are the most pleasing to look at. They are meandering and wavy, thus making the environment feel softer and more natural.</p>\n\n<p>That said, curved lines aren’t restricted to natural environments. We will have one example coming next.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cf7d6e0de80f41a232c9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cf7d6e0de80f41a232c9.webp\" width=\"914\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:547px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Leading lines</h3>\n\n<p>Straight or curved, horizontal or vertical, the continuous nature of lines has a way to lead our eyes seamlessly from where they start to where they end.</p>\n\n<p>Leading lines are a competent tool not only for visual composition but also for navigation. Finding the right prop or the right arrangement of props to form a leading line is a difficult process but utimately rewarding. We will explore this concept further in the next part about repetition.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0ac7c8b5bdb4f65c99fe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0ac7c8b5bdb4f65c99fe.webp\" width=\"891\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:532px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c6b64dd84ac9364e1246.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c6b64dd84ac9364e1246.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The red pipes lead the player to the next area.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2407bb48bb07ef99b067.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2407bb48bb07ef99b067.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1e4743b8d0b39b5f2ba7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1e4743b8d0b39b5f2ba7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d5536398034b3490c5b5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d5536398034b3490c5b5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c71d95678919088d9943.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c71d95678919088d9943.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"651\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/61afbddf3efc739975ca.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/61afbddf3efc739975ca.webp\" width=\"850\" height=\"480\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/38a1f0e6131b0921cfc6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/38a1f0e6131b0921cfc6.webp\" width=\"850\" height=\"431\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dcf73b655c6ea966d5cf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dcf73b655c6ea966d5cf.webp\" width=\"850\" height=\"480\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/90bd74c54d6d646b8e02.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/90bd74c54d6d646b8e02.webp\" width=\"850\" height=\"480\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Repetition</h2>\n\n<p>When an element is repeated multiple times, it creates a pattern or a rhythm, which is pleasing to look at. Besides, repetition is good for conveying distance, scale, and perspective.</p>\n\n<p>A good side effect of patterns is that they <strong>work like leading lines</strong>, guiding the viewer’s eye across the composition, as they pick the repeated elements one by one. In the bottom example, the rocks and trees are placed in a way that leads our eyes forward.</p>\n\n<p>In relation to tech, repetition is a composition element that benefits a lot from modular assets.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/82142f6343eb8b0c8c63.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/82142f6343eb8b0c8c63.webp\" width=\"827\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:494px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Repetition</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe727b8e7f70b4baf27e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe727b8e7f70b4baf27e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/33af978ad69a167b441b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/33af978ad69a167b441b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ecf077475cb4017508da.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ecf077475cb4017508da.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/54d9c222dd1583cc2aa4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/54d9c222dd1583cc2aa4.webp\" width=\"852\" height=\"480\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2732cfef3b9ed888d1ea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2732cfef3b9ed888d1ea.webp\" width=\"852\" height=\"480\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d0cd63cc325cec2ae7fe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d0cd63cc325cec2ae7fe.webp\" width=\"638\" height=\"480\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4de8ec2d6db803776526.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4de8ec2d6db803776526.webp\" width=\"638\" height=\"360\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 41\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<h2>Framing</h2>\n\n<p>Although we can’t control the camera in our levels, we can control how players approach our levels. One common technique for doing that is funneling.</p>\n\n<p>By leading the player through a straight path, such as a corridor or a tunnel, we can almost guarantee that the player will look at what is in front of them. That means we can frame what we want at the end of the tunnel.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/09bc925652ca7d46956c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/09bc925652ca7d46956c.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 42\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>framing</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Bioshock</em> uses a lot of narrow hallways and openings to set the stage.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/952d9e2e5e7c04b9a11b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/952d9e2e5e7c04b9a11b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 43\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>This sequence in <em>The Last of Us 2</em> takes advantage of the level layout to frame the scene.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/29b6f5e93043fed04670.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/29b6f5e93043fed04670.webp\" width=\"684\" height=\"381\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 44\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1c80b247f7852047161f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1c80b247f7852047161f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 45\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>It doesn’t always have to be hallways and corridors, though. There are other “workarounds” for you to achieve the same effect.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/10da57bcb38791c3c60b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/10da57bcb38791c3c60b.webp\" width=\"637\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 46\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c527c2e532931ec50cbb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c527c2e532931ec50cbb.webp\" width=\"637\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 47\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/90e267885a13222f9fe0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/90e267885a13222f9fe0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"505\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 48\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/47aac6bef53cf0932553.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/47aac6bef53cf0932553.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"656\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 49\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1c3e12207991a3730cb7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1c3e12207991a3730cb7.webp\" width=\"564\" height=\"353\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 50\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0e4be9b2187a0313d97b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0e4be9b2187a0313d97b.webp\" width=\"564\" height=\"353\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 51\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a59fccc367dec6305131.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a59fccc367dec6305131.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"501\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 52\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/994463fea8606bc542cb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/994463fea8606bc542cb.webp\" width=\"750\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 53\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Light</h2>\n\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n\n<p>In this section about lighting, we’ll discuss three topics:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Clarity</strong></li><li><strong>Light sources</strong></li><li><strong>Mood</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f2000607af2f81a2b60a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f2000607af2f81a2b60a.webp\" width=\"982\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 54\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Clarity</h2>\n\n<p>The top priority for level lighting is clarity. Players should be able to tell different objects apart. Besides, light should also tell players which direction they should take to progress. We’ll talk about guidance with lighting at a later chapter. For now, let’s focus on using light to <strong>make the environment more readable</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4cec0bbbf419ba863033.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4cec0bbbf419ba863033.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 55\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Light for clarity</strong></p>\n\n<p>In the left picture, the lights allow the silhouettes of different objects read better.</p>\n\n<p>In the right picture, the red light at a distance adds contrast to an otherwise very dark scene. It also helps the area read more clearly.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/246fe9c198b3bda30d32.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/246fe9c198b3bda30d32.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 56\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/abb10c7044371b5bea99.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/abb10c7044371b5bea99.webp\" width=\"828\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 57\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1a0f6808631dc054ced5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1a0f6808631dc054ced5.webp\" width=\"828\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 58\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/92ae1787c2a0317ba82a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/92ae1787c2a0317ba82a.webp\" width=\"702\" height=\"350\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 59\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dd4e9ac788d6bc584974.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dd4e9ac788d6bc584974.webp\" width=\"702\" height=\"350\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 60\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/088c03634227abceb4dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/088c03634227abceb4dd.webp\" width=\"702\" height=\"350\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 61\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong><em><br></em><strong>Catch the light</strong></p>\n\n<p>Our eyes pick up black and white information faster than they do colors. Therefore, when you model something, try to prioritize edges that can potentially interact with light in the environment. It helps the silhouette read better and ground your object in the environment.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cbb7c55e4bc148beb57b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cbb7c55e4bc148beb57b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"381\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 62\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The first thing you need to do is find high-quality references.</p>\n\n<p>Then, you start analyzing the way light hits the object. Here you can see the highlights in the yellow circles.</p>\n\n<p>This is the key to making realistic models (along with shadows).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e75e900b71ccfe665740.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e75e900b71ccfe665740.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"718\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 63\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Note the highlights along the bevels.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2633e54a5ed773a722c0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2633e54a5ed773a722c0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"341\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 64\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><br><br><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/79011577d0dc380fcab6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/79011577d0dc380fcab6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"529\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 65\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/729c79896afddcd79936.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/729c79896afddcd79936.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"461\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 66\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Light sources</h3>\n\n<p>The light source determines the intensity and the color of the light. A few questions to ask about your light source are:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Where does the light come from?</li><li>How many light sources are there?</li><li>Is the light natural light or artificial light? Or a mix of both?</li><li>If it’s natural, what time of day is it?</li></ul>\n\n<p>If it’s artificial, what type of light is it? Electric or fire?</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d25eb28a91249d15d549.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d25eb28a91249d15d549.webp\" width=\"913\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:546px\" alt=\"Illustration 67\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Light source</strong></p>\n\n<p>If your main light source is natural, you should pay attention to the time of day.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/29030b738318f8b639d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/29030b738318f8b639d3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"978\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 68\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>If your level takes place during daytime, you should know what time it is, and how cloudy the sky is.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0ff0a3d2f1a514eb335b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0ff0a3d2f1a514eb335b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"285\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 69\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Mood</h3>\n\n<p>The brightness of an area can affect how the player feels about it. The color of the light is also a contributing factor to the mood of a scene, so let’s talk about color.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e0383f610959705c1a31.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e0383f610959705c1a31.webp\" width=\"1087\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 70\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Mood</strong></p>\n\n<p>Here, we have <em>Demon’s Souls </em>(2009) and <em>Demon’s Souls </em>(2020). Though they are technically the same game, the different lighting direction lends each of them a distinct mood. While the original game feels ominous, the remake feels frightening. They both overwhelm the player, but in two different ways.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c553189b6d2d34fc2901.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c553189b6d2d34fc2901.webp\" width=\"493\" height=\"558\" style=\"--source-width:493px\" alt=\"Illustration 71\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>These screenshots come from <em>God of War (2018)</em>. The different colors of light give each location a distinct look and feel.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3e82353ae423b608d99d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3e82353ae423b608d99d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 72\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Likewise, this one scene from<em> Control</em> elicits a different mood when the lighting changes from one color to another.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/84da2e7150dd5e989e36.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/84da2e7150dd5e989e36.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 73\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Color</h2>\n\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n\n<p>In this section, we will discuss the role of color in visual composition. There will be some overlapping ideas with color in game design, but our main objective remains how to use colors to render eye-pleasing images.</p>\n\n<p>We will go through:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Color hierarchy</strong></li><li><strong>Associative colors</strong></li><li><strong>Transitional colors</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p>If you want to read about color theory, check out <em>Color Fundamentals</em>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7585151ad931cd13e901.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7585151ad931cd13e901.webp\" width=\"598\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 74\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Hierarchy</h3>\n\n<p>Because games are interactive, clarity is very important. The goal of a visual hierarchy is to help players identify key interactions in a level.</p>\n\n<p>The most common way to create color hierarchy is through value (or brightness). Increase the value of important objects, and decrease the values of background objects.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/02304ed8d46f26f8a22e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/02304ed8d46f26f8a22e.webp\" width=\"1008\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 75\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Hierarchy</strong></p>\n\n<p>Climbing is a core mechanic in <em>Uncharted 4</em>. Therefore, the level designers always use a higher value for climbable edges. This way, even when the color has to change due to the surrounding environment, players still can tell whether the action is doable or not.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dbcad0fb2f9f2e773ec0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dbcad0fb2f9f2e773ec0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 76\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>These two boss fights occur in dark areas, so the game adds high-contrast body parts to the bosses to help the player tell them apart from the environments.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/83785667bc0f723849c7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/83785667bc0f723849c7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 77\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Here we have 3 screenshots from <em>Bioshock</em>. Although they use different palettes, they have one thing in common. Each palette only consists of 3 colors at max. And among the 3 chosen colors, there is only 1 dominant, brightest color that defines the main point of focus.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/34a1e119898507150619.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/34a1e119898507150619.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"288\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 78\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9f914ea550b5357c826f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9f914ea550b5357c826f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"570\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 79\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA –</strong> <strong>Yellow is (almost) the brightest color</strong></p>\n\n<p>As said in the first chapter, yellow is the second brightest color, so it is often used for guiding elements.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fba70238cfdde35d79c2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fba70238cfdde35d79c2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 80\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Associative colors</h3>\n\n<p>An associative color is one that is attached to a specific environment. Since there are many different things in a level, it’s better to think in terms of palettes.</p>\n\n<p>Here, we see two different regions in <em>Immortal </em><em>Fenyx</em><em> Rising</em>. The Athena region has a cool palette while the Hephaistos region has a warm palette. This distinction helps create a visual identity for each level in the game, which in turn helps players tell them apart.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4d339404ef788c6ee215.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4d339404ef788c6ee215.webp\" width=\"883\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 81\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Associative colors</strong></p>\n\n<p>Associative colors are also a great tool to make each area in the game look distinct. For example, <em>Journey </em>has a different palette for each location in the game.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b6236b14e0ba55ef4962.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b6236b14e0ba55ef4962.webp\" width=\"522\" height=\"563\" style=\"--source-width:522px\" alt=\"Illustration 82\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Ghost of Tsushima</em> features several different biomes, each with a distinct color palette.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8730c27024212d880376.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8730c27024212d880376.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 83\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Gris</em> uses colors to depict the different stages of grief.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2118389c79afa99a340a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2118389c79afa99a340a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"475\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 84\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Transitional colors</h3>\n\n<p>Transitional colors refer to the change from one color to another that denotes a shift in the story or in a character.</p>\n\n<p>For example, the safe area in <em>Resident Evil 2 Remake</em> is lit by yellow light, while the dangerous areas have dark blue light.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e90e77873c05eaba00c8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e90e77873c05eaba00c8.webp\" width=\"882\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:527px\" alt=\"Illustration 85\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Transitional colors</strong></p>\n\n<p>Similar to <em>Resident Evil 2 Remake</em>, <em>Borderlands 3</em> switches from a greenish hue to a warm yellow once the player steps into this house, signalling that they are now safe from the enemy.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/897b8ed90e0181892461.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/897b8ed90e0181892461.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 86\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The hospital level in <em>The Last of Us 2 </em>changes the lighting to red when the situation becomes more intense. The most interesting part of this sequence is how organic the red light is. Since this level takes place in a hospital, the game just needs to turn on the emergency light to introduce the red light.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f550564b147840ba1555.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f550564b147840ba1555.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 87\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The “Deeply Rooted” mission in <em>It Takes Two</em> has a section that is initially lit by <strong>blue</strong> light coming from jellyfish. Then, <strong>orange</strong> light gets introduced through lamps. Next, <strong>green</strong> fungi enter the scene, and when the player goes deeper into the cave, colorful carvings shine on the walls.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, <strong>neon red </strong>illuminates the path forward as the player reaches the next section in the level. From <strong>blue</strong> to <strong>red</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f45ccb8d6537f90bab52.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f45ccb8d6537f90bab52.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"255\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 88\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
